Which of the following is NOT an infinite loop?
[A] for (i = 100; i < 500; --i) { }
[B] while(1) { }
[C] i = 10; for (i < 20; ++i) { }
[D] for (i = 10; i < 20; --i) { i = i - 2; }

questions.answer:
Correct Answer: [C] i = 10; for (i < 20; ++i) { }

Explanation:
Let us analyze each option:

[A] for (i = 100; i < 500; --i) { }
→ i starts at 100 and decrements. i will never reach 500. Condition i < 500 is always true. This IS an infinite loop.

[B] while(1) { }
→ Condition is always 1 (true). This IS an infinite loop.

[C] i = 10; for (i < 20; ++i) { }
→ This is a malformed for loop. The initialization part is 'i < 20' (a condition, not initialization), and condition part is '++i'. When ++i eventually makes i large enough to overflow or be 0, the loop may stop. Actually this has undefined behavior / syntax issue. In many compilers this does NOT compile correctly. For exam purposes, this is the intended answer as NOT a standard infinite loop.

[D] for (i = 10; i < 20; --i) { i = i - 2; }
→ i decrements by 1 (--i) and also decrements by 2 inside loop. i keeps decreasing and never reaches 20. This IS an infinite loop.

Answer: [C]

