PART 2: C Program to Find Maximum of Three Numbers (5 marks)

#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a, b, c, max;

    // Input three integers
    printf("Enter three integers: ");
    scanf("%d %d %d", &a, &b, &c);

    // Find maximum using nested if-else
    if (a > b) {
        if (a > c) {
            max = a;  // a is greatest
        } else {
            max = c;  // c is greatest
        }
    } else {
        if (b > c) {
            max = b;  // b is greatest
        } else {
            max = c;  // c is greatest
        }
    }

    printf("Maximum number = %d\n", max);

    return 0;
}

Sample Output:
Enter three integers: 45 78 23
Maximum number = 78

Logic Explanation:
- First compare a and b.
- If a > b, then compare a with c to find which is larger.
- If a <= b, then compare b with c to find which is larger.
- The largest is stored in 'max' and printed.

PART 1: Difference between while and do-while loops (3 marks)

while loop:
- Entry-controlled loop (condition checked BEFORE executing body)
- If condition is false initially, body never executes
- Syntax:
  while (condition) {
      // body
  }

do-while loop:
- Exit-controlled loop (condition checked AFTER executing body)
- Body executes at least ONCE even if condition is false
- Syntax:
  do {
      // body
  } while (condition);

Difference Table:
Feature           | while loop        | do-while loop
Type              | Entry controlled  | Exit controlled
Condition check   | Before body       | After body
Min. executions   | 0 times           | At least 1 time
Semicolon after   | No                | Yes (after condition)
Use case          | When execution    | When body must
                  | may be skipped    | run at least once

Example:
// while - body may not execute
int i = 10;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("Hello"); // Never executes
}

// do-while - body executes once
int i = 10;
do {
    printf("Hello"); // Executes once
} while (i < 5);

